English is a worldwide language and if  you want to live function and thrive in  this globalized world you have to be  able to speak it fluently so one might  conclude that everybody native English  speakers in non-native English speakers  consider English to be an indispensable  useful efficient tool to make  communication in general much easier but  the reality is there are some people who  are not that satisfied about English  having this worldwide status for a  number of reasons and they may have a  point  hi my name is Luca from Lucca Lampard  comm and today we're going to explore  whether English is actually getting in  the way of learning other foreign  languages let's start by exploring and  taking a look at some of the  disadvantages or obstacles that people  might have when it comes to practicing  learning foreign languages and let's  start with the perspective of native  English speakers within the group of  native English speakers Australians  Americans Brits there is a group that  never leaves the country and who will  always live in their original country  for most native English speakers who  never move abroad while learning a  foreign language is not that important  even if they have to deal with tourists  coming to their own country well they do  it in their own native language because  they expect everybody to speak English  so living in your own country never  moving abroad and on top of that having  English as your mother tongue well most  often than not it keeps you from even  starting learning foreign languages so  let's consider native English speakers  who actually move abroad and live abroad  for a long period of time well for a lot  of them speaking a foreign language is  not an option because first they think  that learning the local language is too  difficult and second people local people  might speak English and third they live  within the so called expats bubble which  allows them to speak English all the  time without necessarily resorting to  the local language or any other language  for that  matter so living in their own country  where everybody speaks English possibly  having to do with tourists who speak  their own language and not really having  to learn other foreign languages if not  at school  bits and pieces of French Spanish German  or whatnot well that's the perfect  combination for I don't really need to  speak other languages okay now we've  taken a look at the lazy categories of  native English speakers but what about  the native English speakers who actually  do want to learn a foreign language be  it because they're really interested in  and they live in their own country or  because they move abroad and they really  want to blend in they really want to  integrate in the local life in this case  even if a native English speaker has an  ironclad will to speak their local  language well all sorts of things can  actually happen that do not depend on  them let's consider mark an American guy  living in New York who suddenly decides  to move to the Netherlands after  learning Dutch for two years he goes  there tries to interact with native  speakers he's really willing to use  Dutch as much as he can and much to his  dismay he actually finds out that most  of the time Dutch people tend to reply  to him in English as you know in Sweden  a Norway or the Netherlands for that  matter people speak so well speak  English so well that they tend to use it  and actually think they're doing you a  favor by switching back to English maybe  because you're Dutch you're in a region  or you're Swedish is not still that good  so in this case English does get a  little bit in the way be it because your  level in the foreign language you're  trying to practice is not good enough or  because the English of the local people  is actually too good and the funny thing  is that this does not only happen with  locals who master English and speak  English extremely well this actually  happens to native English speakers who  try to practice a local language with  locals who want to practice their  English because their English is not  good enough so the fact that English is  a worldwide language and everybody  speaks it or wants to speak it sometimes  gets in the way of you learning a  foreign language  and let's consider one final category  there's actually a lot more categories  but we're just considering the main ones  that is none native English speakers who  want to practice the target language  what happens then  well again a lot of things can happen  they can talk to a local person they  meet in a shop on the market wherever  and this person happens to want to speak  and practice English or their level is  not good enough they don't feel  confident maybe they're a little bit  tired or they're just lazy and they tend  to resort to English by myself and I  will tell you in a second about my  personal story and guilty sometimes of  resorting to English and I'm not doing  the right thing and we're doing the easy  thing just because I am lazy so let's  take Mario a guy from Rome who decides  to move to Greece and he wants to  practice Greek as much as he can again a  number of things can happen let's  suppose that mario meets a person on the  market and speaks greek and that person  replies in english because he wants to  practice english or let's suppose that  mario wants to take lessons on skype for  example and he wants to practice greek  sometimes it happens that mario is a  little bit lazy he might be a little bit  tired and he himself resource to english  or a Studer maybe thinks that his level  of greek is not good enough and he  speaks english constantly trying to  explain greek patterns and greek  grammars and whatnot so there's a wide  range of reasons that depend on you that  depend on the situation that depend on  the person you were talking about for  which unfortunately English kind of gets  in the way of learning foreign languages  so mark on the one hand and Mari on the  other are just two very simple examples  of what can happen and how English can  actually get in the way of learning  foreign languages unfortunately we do  not have time to explore all the  possible situations but this was just to  give you an idea of what can happen to  you and what can happen to me or anybody  else for that matter when it comes to  learning and practicing foreign  languages  so you might think that English is that  kind of bully that gets into the picture  you're taking a picture for yourself and  this guy pops up it hey I'm there I'm  always there but the reality is that  it's not all doom and gloom as they say  in fact having English around as a  lingua franca has a lot of benefits so  let's meet Francois a Parisian who  decides to move to China he doesn't  speak a word of Chinese when he lives in  China he meets a very interesting  Chinese girl who does not speak a word  of French but they both speak English so  they can actually start communicating in  English and then later they can learn  each other's language that has happened  so many times across the world and it's  a great benefit and it's a great  blessing to have a language through  which you can immediately communicate  another benefit of having English as a  lingua franca and this is especially for  language buffs like me is that language  learning material for rarer languages is  most often than not just in English so  you can leverage the knowledge that you  have in English or any other lingua  franca like French or Russian to learn  other languages that you would not be  able to learn if you just didn't speak  English English is also very very  beneficial when it comes to using  translation in order to learn a foreign  language you can translate back and  forth you can compare structures with  the target language between the target  language and the English language and  there's a lot of learning materials that  have English on the one side and the  target language on the other side  English is also very fast and efficient  too when it comes to extracting  information from your language tutor or  language partner the person you were  talking to but suppose that you are  Italian and you're learning Greek and  you're talking to your Greek tutor and  you don't know how to say something well  you just ask how do you say this in  Greek and you say this in English and  the other person very often speaks  English well and can immediately deliver  the answer that's a very fast way of  communicating and filling in the gaps by  using English on and off of course some  purists say that it would be better to  stick to just to the target language and  that is obviously preferred  but I don't think that that is a  disadvantage another example of how  English can be beneficial is that I for  one and making this video exactly in  English some of you sometimes complain  that I should make more videos in other  languages truth be told but making these  videos in English easily allows me to  reach a very very wide audience and talk  practically to everyone my personal take  on this is that English is neither bad  or good and I always tend to use an ends  logic more than an or logic I think that  it's great to use English and other  languages it's not English or other  languages sometimes I myself will decide  to use English a little bit more  sometimes my interlocutor will reply in  English it does not matter this still  hasn't kept me from using all the  languages that I've been learning in the  ears and I think that it all boils down  to attitude and decisions if sometimes  someone replies you in English you can  gently say look I've been learning this  language Dutch be a Dutch Greek or any  other language for some time and it will  really practice it sometimes it just  boils down to conveying the message and  just telling your interlocutor this is  what I really want to do it can do  wonders  referring to what I said before about  the disadvantages and/or advantages of  using English as a lingua franca a lot  of times English has gotten a little bit  in the way for example I myself am  guilty sometimes of resorting to English  when I'm not confident enough in the  foreign language or when I'm too tired  or again when I'm lazy be it in  conversations on skype or in real life  surprisingly in a lot of circumstances  in countries I do not like speaking the  local language in shops or in brief  interactions when I have to buy  something at the supermarket and the  local shop etc I don't really know why  but I do remember that I was in Poland I  was with Richard Simpkins many of you I  know Richard Simchat he's a fantastic  polyglot and once I just kept using  English while the main goal of being in  Poland was actually speaking polish and  he looked at me and said we've got is  there a problem with this and I said no  there's no problem it's just that I  don't find these short interactions very  meaningful while for him that was that  made his day interacting with these  people he that's reason why he went  there he ate there because he wanted to  practice the language also in these  situations and this also happens when I  have conversations in Hungarian or Greek  languages I'm actively learning  languages I'm eager to speak as much as  I can with my tutors sometimes I resort  to English to explain things that are  really difficult or sometimes I don't  have a word and I asked directly in  English so I would say that Oh in all  having English in my life has been a  great benefit I've never read it  speaking English and to this day I  really like speaking it be it with  non-native or a native English speakers  it has never kept me from learning other  languages it actually helped me learn  other languages and sometimes I have to  accept that you know using English when  you're a little bit too tired not  confident and whatnot is absolutely  natural again it all boils down to  - and now a couple of questions for you  how has English impacted you language  learning is English your native language  or a foreign language how have you used  English to learn foreign languages  have you ever stumbled upon people are  really proud and really stubborn about  speaking English instead of letting you  practice language you want to practice  please feel free to share all your  experiences all your methods in the  comment section below I will take some  of the comments I will read them out  loud and I will reply in a video once  again thanks a lot for watching and see  you very very soon here on YouTube  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

